--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Part A - Using seeded games
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  +------------------------+
  | A1. What a seed gives  |
  +------------------------+

A seed is set either by the player:

      OPTIONS=seed:7394023815          (in .nethackrc)
      NETHACKOPTIONS=seed:12345 nethack

or by the server for every new game, which also hides it from players:

      SEED=1556329103471883006         (in sysconf)

A seed is a number or any text of at most 63 characters without commas
(B1). Everyone who starts a game with the same seed gets:

  o the same character: the seed picks role, race, gender and alignment
    (the player's role options are ignored, and the welcome message says so)

  o the same starting inventory, attributes and pet (no rerolls, no
    tutorial)

  o the same object identities (a "bubbly potion" is the same potion for
    everyone)

  o the same dungeon structure (branch locations, special level variants)

  o the same map, traps, objects and monsters on every level

  o on each level, the same chance of a wandering monster on a given turn,
    and the same species if one turns up

This holds whatever each player did beforehand, whatever order they visit
levels in (stairs, trapdoors, level teleport), and whatever experience level
they arrive at. Combat and everything else decided during play stays random.

A seeded game also plays a little differently from NetHack 5.0, the same way
for everyone:

  o levels, wandering monsters and the Astral Plane's player-monsters are
    made for a stand-in hero whose experience level is the level's depth
    (20 anywhere in the endgame), with no Luck (B4.4): a hero who dives
    ahead of their experience level meets tougher monsters than in NetHack
    5.0 (the difficulty cap, the size of groups and the enchantment of
    objects go by the stand-in's level), and one who lingers meets weaker
    ones

  o the real date and time don't count (B6): the moon is always between new
    and full, there's no Friday the 13th, and it's never night or midnight,
    so gremlins never steal intrinsics, werecreatures change form as they do
    by day, and undead never hit harder at midnight

  o no bones files are left or loaded, the local high score list is never
    used (for player statues, graveyard corpses, doppelgangers' forms), and
    a Samurai's splint mail found on a level is lacquered only in the Quest

  o in a recorded game (A3, B11) there's no shell escape, no suspending and
    no mail, and the game doesn't follow a change of the terminal's size

Without a seed, the game behaves like NetHack 5.0, and its save and bones
files are interchangeable with NetHack 5.0's. The one difference is that two
sorts break ties, so every platform orders rooms and monster types the same
way where NetHack 5.0's order depends on the platform's qsort() (B4.6).


  +-------------------------+
  | A2. What still differs  |
  +-------------------------+

  o During play: combat rolls, monster AI, and anything else rolled during
    play.

  o Wandering monsters: where one is placed (it depends on the hero's
    position), its inventory, and which spawns a racer sees (racers reach a
    level at different turns). Once the Wizard of Yendor has first been
    killed, or the invocation done (u.uevent.udemigod), they turn up more
    often. The timeline can be learned from a practice game.

  o Player history, limited to the monsters and objects concerned:
      - a genocided species is simply missing (every choice is made as if
        nothing were genocided, then a genocided monster is left out); its
        special rooms are left empty and a shapeshifter can't take its form
      - uniques already made aren't made again, whether or not they were
        killed: a demon prince summoned earlier is missing from his lair
        (another monster may take the square, e.g. a king on a throne)
      - where a monster is missing, monsters nearby may be placed on other
        squares, and one that would have been turned away from a square
        (e.g. a shop's mimic) may find it free
      - artifacts that already exist can't be made again, so another
        artifact (or a plain object) may be made in its place
      - what a missing monster would have been made with (its inventory, a
        hider's object to hide under, a giant spider's web) is missing too,
        or left on the floor where a special level puts it

  o Aggravate monster and the Amulet: a hero wearing a ring of aggravate
    monster or carrying the Amulet gets tougher monsters (only the monsters,
    including wandering ones) on a new level. The penalty stays, but can't
    change the map or other items; what comes with a monster (its inventory,
    a hider's object to hide under) comes with the tougher one.

  o The hero's gear, for the monsters it concerns: wielding Excalibur or
    Demonbane makes demon princes hostile, wielding a bec de corbin makes
    ravens peaceful, and a hero who can see invisible meets peaceful
    monsters that may carry invisibility (none of this reaches the map,
    objects, or monsters inside objects such as statues).

  o Alignment: monsters are made peaceful or not for the hero's starting
    alignment, so a hero who has converted meets monsters peaceful to their
    old god (what killing one does to the hero's alignment goes by the new
    one).

  o Orcish Town: its raiders are placed with the usual randomness when they
    arrive on later levels.

  o Visit order: it can decide which vault level gets the Fort Ludios
    portal.

  o Object ids: some things depend on o_id (e.g. the shop price surcharge on
    o_id % 4, the helm of opposite alignment).

  o Installation: different builds or data files (rumors, engravings,
    epitaphs), the ROGUEOPTS environment variable (Rogue level), and wizard
    mode (it generates special rooms more readily, forces the Fort Ludios
    roll's result, and changes some dungeon-structure choices in dungeon.c).

  o Handicap options: pauper, nudist and explore mode change the starting
    kit (attributes and everything else stay the same, since attributes have
    their own stream).


  +---------------------+
  | A3. Running a race  |
  +---------------------+

  o Scouting: knowing the seed means being able to scout; a practice game
    shows every map, object identity and shop, and the wandering monster
    timeline. Either announce the seed when the race starts and let
    everyone start together (an open seed race), or hide it (below).

  o Hidden seed: set SEED in sysconf. What has to stay secret is the
    dungeon, not the text of the seed, and SEED applies to every new game
    on that installation, so:
      - give the race an installation of its own (its own sysconf and
        playground, e.g. its own dgamelaunch entry), open only to the
        registered racers, with EXPLORERS limited to the operators
      - count only each racer's first game with that seed, however it
        ended and in whatever mode (look for the first xlogfile record
        with seedhidden=1 and the race's seed=), since a second game is the
        same dungeon; from the moment a game shows anything of its hero or
        dungeon, a hangup saves it, and a recorded game's record has it, so
        a racer can't look and quietly start again
      - use a long random seed, never reused (e.g. from
        "od -An -N8 -tu8 /dev/urandom"): nothing the game shows (the digest,
        the level fingerprints, the levels themselves) can be traced back
        to the seed, but a short or guessable seed can be found by trying
        guesses against what's shown; against the digest or the
        fingerprints each guess takes about a tenth of a second (B1), but
        against the levels themselves (say, a shopkeeper's name) far less
      - check that the game starts: a SEED line that isn't a valid seed
        stops every new game (with a message that doesn't show it) rather
        than let players use their own seeds
      - don't let players download their save files (the seed is in them)
      - don't let players watch each other live, and hold back dumplogs,
        ttyrecs and records until the race is over: they show maps and
        object identities, which are the same for everyone
      - xlogfile's seed= then holds the digest ("hidden#1a2b3c4d"), not the
        seed, and seedhidden=1 marks it as the server's (a player's own
        seed can't start with "hidden#"); start a test game to see the
        race's digest
      - a racer with a saved game from before carries on with that game:
        tell racers to finish old games first; a record without seed= (or
        with another one) isn't a race game
      - keep the race's sysconf (or its SEED) after the race: replaying a
        record needs the seed (B11)

  o Same installation: everyone must use the same build of this fork and the
    same data files (rumors, engravings and epitaphs feed some random
    choices), with the ROGUEOPTS environment variable unset, and not in
    wizard mode.

  o Verifying: only records written by the server (xlogfile, dumplog,
    ttyrec, the game's record) verify a game; a game played on a player's
    own machine can't be verified, and a modified game could print
    anything. To check a game:
      - seed=, seedhidden=, seedver=, build= and datahash= in xlogfile must
        match the race's, and its flags field (wizard and explore mode are
        marked there, and blind, deaf, nudist and pauper in the conduct
        fields) must be allowed; build= is the git commit the game was
        built from ("-dirty" if tracked files had uncommitted changes; the
        version and build time if it wasn't built from git), and datahash=
        covers the data files that affect the levels (DLB builds only;
        otherwise "unknown")
      - the dumplog's level fingerprints confirm the build and seed:
        compare them with a reference; terrain should always match, and
        traps too apart from the Fort Ludios portal and a web under a
        monster that differs; objects and monsters can differ for the
        reasons in A2
      - for a reference, an operator who knows the seed can start a game
        with it as their own seed option, in explore mode, on another
        installation running with the operator's own permissions (not a
        setgid one), with NH_SEEDFUZZ set to a new file's name (see B10):
        the game writes every level's fingerprint (the "F" lines of the
        first pass, which are the dumplog's lines with an "F" in front)
        and exits; never use wizard mode, which makes some levels
        differently
      - with RECORDFILE set in sysconf, every seeded game played with the
        tty interface is recorded, in a build with DUMPLOG (the Linux build
        hints define it; the macOS ones leave it commented out), and
        "nethack --replay FILE --verify" plays it again and checks it
        (B11): a game whose replay checks out was played with exactly
        those keys, on that build, and nothing else happened in it

  o Rules to decide: whether the race is timed by turns or by real time, and
    whether blind, deaf, nudist and pauper are allowed as declared handicaps.

  o Dumplogs need DUMPLOG compiled in (the Linux build hints do this) and
    DUMPLOGFILE set in sysconf.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Part B - Changes
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  +----------------+
  | B1. The seed   |
  +----------------+

Files: include/optlist.h, src/options.c, src/rnd.c, src/cfgfiles.c,
src/allmain.c, sys/{unix,windows,share,vms,libnh}/*main.c,
sys/unix/sysconf, dat/opthelp, doc/Guidebook.*

  o Option: seed (NHOPTC, set_gameview) can only be set at startup;
    optfn_seed() also rejects it outside initial option processing, so e.g.
    nh.parse_config can't change it. "!seed", or "seed:" with no value,
    turns off a seed set earlier (e.g. one from the options file, by
    NETHACKOPTIONS).

  o Value: whatever it came from (the options file, NETHACKOPTIONS,
    sysconf), the text is made canonical first (seed_canonical()): spaces
    and tabs around it are dropped, and each run of them inside it becomes
    one space. It can be at most 63 characters (SEEDSZ - 1) after that, and
    mustn't hold a control character, DEL, '=' or ',' (so it's safe for
    xlogfile, and can be given as an option, which a comma would end); a
    player's own seed can't start with "hidden#" (in any case), what a
    server's hidden seed is shown as. An all-digit value (up to 19 digits,
    leading zeros dropped, so "0042" is "42") is used as a number; any other
    text is hashed with FNV-1a 64 (seed_value()).

  o Server seed: SEED= in sysconf (cnf_line_SEED(), nh_set_server_seed())
    sets the seed of every new game. Sysconf is read first, and
    nh_seed_option() then ignores the player's own seed option (checking it
    all the same; the welcome message says it was ignored). A SEED line
    whose value isn't a valid seed (an empty one included) isn't reported
    where it's read, since every player would see it, and doesn't let the
    players' own seeds be used instead: no new game starts
    (refuse_new_game_bad_seed(), before the player chooses a character,
    and in newgame()), and the message says only that sysconf's SEED isn't
    valid. Restoring a game still works (it has its own seed). Such a seed is
    hidden: nh_seed_display() shows "hidden#<8 hex digits>" (a digest of
    the seed) wherever the seed is shown, including xlogfile's seed=. The
    real seed is still what's saved, with the fact that it's hidden (B7).

  o What's derived from the seed can't be traced back to it:
      - sub-seeds come from nh_seed_for(tag, n1, n2), which is keyed_mix():
        splitmix64 over the seed, the tag's hash and two numbers, with the
        seed mixed in again after that, so undoing the mixing leaves the
        seed on both sides of the equation, and a sub-seed (with its public
        name) gives it back only to someone who guesses it
      - the seed's key-stretching chain (seed_chain_end()): 2^25 rounds of
        splitmix64, each needing the one before and taking the seed again,
        so it can't be shortened or run backwards; about a tenth of a
        second, made once per seeded game (hidden or not), when first
        needed (gseed.chain)
      - from the end of the chain, with a constant each: the digest shown
        for a hidden seed, and the key of the level fingerprints
        (nh_levelhash_salt(), B8); so even someone who worked the key out
        from the fingerprints shown would only have the chain's end, and
        checking a guess of the seed against the digest or the
        fingerprints takes the whole chain, about ten guesses a second per
        processor
    A long random seed is what makes a hidden seed safe (A3).

  o #saveoptions writes the player's own seed option, not the seed of a
    restored game or the server's.

  o ISAAC64 is seeded from exactly 8 bytes, independent of sizeof (long).

  o Seeded games need USE_ISAAC64 (the default). Other builds reject the
    option (and treat a sysconf SEED as invalid); they still compile, with
    the stream functions as no-ops.


  +---------------------+
  | B2. Random streams  |
  +---------------------+

Files: src/rnd.c

      void rng_stream_begin(const char *tag, long n1, long n2);
      void rng_stream_end(void);

  o A stream temporarily replaces the CORE rng's state (seeding it from the
    seed), so every rn2(), rnd(), rnl(), d(), rne() and rnz() call, and
    Lua's nh.random()/nh.rn2() (which nhlib.lua's math.random shim uses),
    draws from it without further changes. Lua's pairs() is only used where
    no rng is drawn.

  o It's a stack, 32 deep; an unbalanced end is reported with impossible().
    All of these are no-ops when not seeded.

  o Play stays random: the CORE and DISP rngs are seeded from entropy as
    usual, and reseed_random() works as usual, including around level
    creation and on level return. Only the named streams come from the
    seed, so play (combat, prayer, fountains and so on) can't be practised
    and replayed, and restoring doesn't reset the rng to a known state.


  +----------------+
  | B3. New game   |
  +----------------+

Files: src/allmain.c, src/role.c, src/u_init.c, src/options.c

newgame() runs each part of setup in its own stream:

      init_objects()                    "objects": appearance shuffle
      seed_force_identity()             "identity": role, race, gender,
                                        alignment
      role_init()                       "role": pantheon, quest data
      init_dungeons()                   "dungeon": branch placement,
                                        special level variants
      init_artifacts(), u_init_misc()   "misc"
      makedog()                         "pet" (pettype is ignored)
      u_init_inventory_attrs() ...      "hero" (rerolling is disabled);
        u_init_skills_discoveries()     init_attr()/vary_init_attr() inside
                                        it use "attrs", so a different
                                        starting kit can't change attributes

  o Seed: newgame() starts from the seed sysconf and the options set, as
    noted when option processing ended (nh_seed_options_done(),
    nh_seed_new_game()), so a restore that failed partway can't leave its
    seed behind (B7).

  o Character: seed_force_identity() runs at the end of option processing
    (so the selection dialog is skipped) and again in newgame(), before
    role_init() (so -p/-r can't override it). With the player's role
    filters cleared, it picks a valid role, race, gender and alignment. If
    the player's own options asked for something else, the welcome message
    says they were ignored.

  o Nothing is shown before the game can be saved: a seeded game makes
    everything first (inventory, skills, a wizard-mode wizkit), then marks
    itself as worth saving (newgame_saveable()), and only then draws the
    map and status and shows the legacy text. So from the moment anything
    about the new hero or dungeon is shown, a hangup saves the game (the
    real-time clock starts again after the legacy text, as usual). An
    unseeded game keeps NetHack 5.0's order.

  o Tutorial: never offered in a seeded game (ask_do_tutorial()); it would
    show some object appearances before the race.


  +-------------------------+
  | B4. Level generation    |
  +-------------------------+

B4.1 A layout stream per level (src/mklev.c)

  o mklev() calls rng_level_begin(ledger), so the level's layout (rooms,
    corridors, stairs, doors, traps, room types, special-level script
    choices) comes from ("layout", ledger). The ledger layout is fixed at
    game start, so visit order doesn't matter.

B4.2 A stream per monster and object (src/rnd.c, src/makemon.c,
     src/mkobj.c, src/sp_lev.c, src/mkroom.c, src/mklev.c, src/trap.c,
     src/mkmaze.c, src/shknam.c, src/priest.c, src/teleport.c)

  o What gets one: everything made along the way: makemon(), rndmonst_adj(),
    mkclass_aligned(), mksobj(), mkobj(), rndmonnum() (an object's species),
    mkcorpstat() (including a corpse's rot timer), statue traps, special
    levels' des.monster() (with its inventory function) and des.object()
    (and a class-based montype), the species choice for special-room
    monsters, graveyard undead, mimic doors, the niche corpse's species,
    each Orcish Town gang member with its gear, and monsters moved or
    removed during generation (the shopkeeper's and priest's "insurance"
    rloc(), swamp pools, Baalzebub's lair, Orcus Town's shopkeepers).
    rng_content_enter()/rng_content_leave() do the switching, and nested
    calls (a monster's inventory, box contents) share the outer one's
    stream.

  o Keys: a stream is keyed by how many draws the layout had made at that
    point, plus a counter for several at the same point:
    ("monsters" or "objects", ledger + 4096 * n, layout draws). So a monster
    or object that comes out differently (a genocided species, an artifact
    that already exists) changes only itself, never the layout or any other
    monster or object, and it doesn't matter if one is skipped. Only draws
    from the layout stream itself are counted; nested streams, e.g. loading
    themes, aren't. A stream that is only sometimes needed (e.g. moving a
    monster only if one is in the way) is entered every time, so the
    counter for its layout position stays the same.

  o Kind: a nested one is made as what it is: an object that is part of a
    monster (inventory, a hider's object to hide under) is made as an
    object, and a monster made as part of an object (the template for a
    statue's contents) as an object too. Species picks inherit the kind.
    This decides whether the aggravate/Amulet difficulty applies (B4.4).
    rng_making_level_layout() is true while the layout or an object is
    being made; rng_making_monster_part() while a monster (not one inside
    an object), or something made as part of it, is.

  o Placement: placing a monster or object (which can take more tries when
    a square is taken) uses a sub-stream of its own, so where it lands
    can't change anything else about it (rng_placement_begin() and
    rng_placement_end(), in makemon() and des.monster()/des.object()).

  o Parts: each member of a monster's group (m_initgrp()) is made in a
    sub-stream of its own (rng_part_begin()), so a member that can't be
    placed doesn't change the others.

  o Sub-streams are named by what they're for, the kind of the monster or
    object (a monster and an object made at the same point share their key)
    and a count, e.g. ("placement:monster:0", key).

B4.3 Genocide and extinction (src/makemon.c; checks in src/mon.c,
     src/monmove.c, src/polyself.c, src/zap.c, src/wizard.c, src/timeout.c,
     src/insight.c, src/end.c, src/read.c, src/do.c, src/allmain.c)

  o While a seeded level is made, every choice acts as though no species
    were genocided or extinct (uniques already made excepted):
    seeded_fresh_species() gives the level a fresh view of mvitals
    (clearing G_GONE, and the G_NOCORPSE genocide adds), recording
    extinctions from births meanwhile in the real flags. Checks during
    generation that read mvitals directly (e.g. a quest level's enemy
    species, a tin's contents) see this view.

  o makemon() consults the real flags (species_genocided()) to refuse a
    genocided monster, after its usual retries for a square. So only the
    genocided monsters themselves go missing; an extinct species is still
    made. Shapeshifters' forms use the real flags too.

  o While the layout or an object (including a monster made as part of one,
    like a statue's petrified monster) is being made, species_genocided()
    says nothing is genocided: genocide doesn't change what statues there
    are.

  o Gameplay checks that must see real genocides (a shapeshifter's forms,
    polymorph, a vampire's rising, slime, the genocide list shown at the
    end and so on) use species_genocided(), which gives the real flags
    outside generation.

B4.4 Stand-in hero (src/rnd.c, src/dungeon.c, src/makemon.c)

While mklev() runs, and while choosing a wandering monster or making the
Astral Plane's player-monsters, these hero fields are saved, replaced, and
restored afterwards:

      field               stand-in value        why
      u.ulevel            depth (1-30); 20      monster difficulty cap,
                          anywhere in the       group sizes, adj_lev(), rne()
                          endgame (In_endgame)
      u.ualign.type       u.ualignbase[         peacefulness (helm of
                          A_ORIGINAL]           opposite alignment)
      u.ualign.record     10                    peace_minded() odds
      u.ualign.abuse      0                     peace_minded()
      u.uluck, u.moreluck 0                     rnl()
      u.ux, u.uy          0 (level creation     the old position is from
                          only)                 the previous level
      protection from     off                   changes how shapeshifters
      shape changers                            and mimics are made
      hallucination       off                   messages made while
                                                generating draw numbers

  o Difficulty bumps: level_difficulty() ignores aggravate monster and
    carrying the Amulet while a seeded level's layout and objects are made
    (rng_making_level_layout()). Its monsters, and wandering monsters, still
    get them. makemon()'s own Amulet checks (sleeping nymphs, peacefulness)
    follow the same rule (gen_hero_has_amulet()).

  o Afterwards: each monster's alignment weight (what killing it does to the
    hero's alignment, set_malign()) is recomputed for the real hero, for a
    new level (mklev()) and for the Astral Plane's player-monsters
    (astral_mplayers()); and if the real hero has protection from shape
    changers, the new level's shapeshifters and mimics revert as they would
    on a level being returned to (rescham(), in goto_level()).

  o Hero's gear: wielding Excalibur, Demonbane or a bec de corbin and seeing
    invisible affect only the monsters concerned (makemon.c, muse.c), not
    monsters made as part of an object.

  o Panic save: seeded_gen_cancel(), called from dosave0(), puts the real
    hero back and unwinds the streams first, in case a panic happens
    mid-generation.

B4.5 Layout choices that depended on game state

Each of these comes out the same when seeded:

  o Special rooms (mklev.c, mkroom.c): a genocided species doesn't make the
    room choice fall through to the next room type; the room is made and
    its spots for those monsters stay empty rather than getting random
    monsters (an anthole with all ants gone stays empty).

  o Ordinary rooms (mklev.c): carrying the Amulet doesn't give every room a
    monster (normally 1 in 3 get one).

  o Shops (shknam.c): the mimic roll is made even where a monster already
    stands, and genocided mimics leave the spot empty; every bookstore gets
    a novel, so the roll is always made; shopkeepers' names are based on the
    seed and the shopkeeper's square, not its monster id (which depends on
    how many monsters and objects have been made).

  o Shopkeepers and priests (shknam.c, priest.c, teleport.c): a monster
    standing where one is to be made is moved away; if it's still there
    (it couldn't be moved, or the move picked the same spot) it's removed
    (rloc_out_of_the_way()). In vanilla the shop or temple is then left
    without its keeper, and a shop unstocked, which would change the rest
    of the level for whoever had a different monster there.

  o Mimic doors (mklev.c): genocided mimics leave an empty doorway.

  o Fort Ludios portal (mklev.c): the 1-in-3 roll is always made.

  o Artifacts (mkobj.c): the chance is computed as if no artifacts existed.

  o High scores (topten.c): a seeded game never reads the local high score
    list (get_rnd_toptenentry() gives none), which differs from one
    installation, and one moment, to the next: player statues and
    graveyard corpses (made with a level, or dug up during play) get a
    random role instead, and so does a doppelganger taking a player's form;
    the calls' own random numbers are drawn as before, so what follows
    doesn't shift.

  o Samurai splint mail (mkobj.c): one made with a level is lacquered only
    in the Quest, not by the turn the level is made on (a Samurai's own
    starting armour is lacquered as usual).

  o Swamps (mkroom.c): pools don't skip squares with a monster or object on
    them.

  o Defensive items (muse.c, rnd_defensive_item()): during generation only
    the level's own no-teleport flag counts, not which demon lords are
    about.

  o Things finished last (mklev.c): webs under giant spiders, and long
    worms' tails (worm.c, grow_worm_tail()), are added once the level is
    otherwise done, a tail from its own ("wormtail", ledger, position)
