  o build= (build_id()) is the git commit the game was built from
    (nomakedefs.git_sha; the Unix hints add "-dirty" if the tree had local
    changes), or else the version and build time. It's the build that
    wrote the record, which may not be the one that made every level. It's
    compiled into date.o, which make doesn't always rebuild, so after
    changing or committing sources, remove src/date.o (or make clean)
    before building a race's binary, or build= can name an older state.

  o datahash= (data_files_hash()) is an FNV-1a hash of the names and
    contents of the data library's files, in name order, leaving out the
    help texts and the list of compile-time options, which don't affect
    the levels (dlb_member_name() in dlb.c lists the files). It's "unknown"
    without DLB.

  o #levelhash (do_levelhash(); type it in full, autocomplete doesn't offer
    it) works in seeded games (and wizard mode) and prints the level's
    fingerprint as generated: terrain, traps, objects, monsters. Each part
    starts from a value keyed by the end of the seed's chain and the level
    (nh_levelhash_salt(), B1), so a fingerprint can't be looked up in a
    table made with other seeds (e.g. to tell which variant of a fixed
    special level this is), nor traced back to the seed. Only what was
    generated goes in: a slime mold's fruit id (the player's fruit option)
    isn't included, and neither is an object's corpsenm unless it's a
    species (corpses, statues, figurines, eggs, tins) or a novel's title,
    since elsewhere it can hold a monster id (a saddle's or leash's
    leashmon), which depends on how many monsters and objects were made
    before. The ledger number, which would give away the main dungeon's
    length on a branch level, and the current values are shown only in
    wizard mode. Fingerprints are saved with the game, so they survive a
    restore.


