  +-----------------------+
  | C2. How it's checked  |
  +-----------------------+

On every push, CI (.github/workflows/ci.yml) builds the game with the
Linux and macOS hints from a clean tree (so build= is honest), on Linux
x86-64 and aarch64 and macOS x86-64 and arm64; on Linux x86-64
it runs the history fuzzer (B10, test/seedfuzz.py) over a few dozen
seeds and the recording tests (B11, test/replaytest.py, with and without
signals); on every platform it builds sfctool, packages a release
tarball and, with the compiled-in playground removed, makes every level
of one seed from the unpacked tarball. A v* tag publishes the tarballs.

What the checks establish:

  o The fuzzer makes every level under each perturbation in B10 and allows
    only that perturbation's documented differences, the fingerprints and
    each level's wandering monster timeline included. It does catch leaks:
    hashing an object's monster-id field into the fingerprint, or reading
    the high score list for a doppelganger's form, fails it.

  o A replay checks out only if every key, system seed and signal of the
    recorded game reproduces every checkpoint digest; malformed records
    (truncated, wrong lengths, unknown entries, out-of-range values) are
    rejected.

Checked by hand, not by CI:

  o A killed game: a session ended with SIGKILL, the recover utility run
    on its checkpoint files, and the game restored and finished, replays
    as "cut off" then "verified", the replay recovering the files itself
    between the two sessions.

  o Word size and libc don't matter: a 32-bit musl build (another qsort())
    writes byte for byte the same fuzzer output as 64-bit glibc.

  o AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer builds run the fuzzer,
    the replay tests, and recovery of a game killed with SIGKILL (seeded
    or not, with or without a seed option or sysconf SEED) cleanly.

  o Save files: an unseeded game's save restores in NetHack 5.0 and vice
    versa; NetHack 5.0 refuses a seeded game's save ("Configuration
    incompatibility"); both convert through sfctool and back; a level
    made after a restore has the fingerprint it would have had without
    one; a save whose seed text was tampered with doesn't restore.

  o Hidden seeds: the digest and the fingerprints of a game under a
    sysconf SEED match a reference game given the same seed as a player's
    option; an attack that recovers a seed from fingerprints salted with a
    plain sub-seed finds nothing against the chained salt, and one guess
    against a fingerprint costs the whole chain.

  o Records: a new game never overwrites an existing record or writes
    through a symbolic link; a restored game whose record is a link is
    saved again and ends; a hangup at the legacy text, in a long search,
    and ^C between turns all replay; a hidden-seed record replays with
    --seed or --sysconf after SEED changed, and refuses to start with no
    seed at all; games of thousands of random keys over several sessions
    replay.

Not yet run anywhere: Windows. CI only builds it (MSYS2, x64, from
sys/windows/build-msys2.txt), allowed to fail, and keeps the result out
of the releases.
